      "updated_at": "2026-05-25T06:15:16Z",
      "body": "The build info now slides out from a git icon on the bottom right, animated and styled to be similar to the cursor inspector:\r\n\r\n<img width=\"433\" height=\"99\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-05-24 at 11 09 11\u202fPM\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9d6066f6-a0b9-4c50-a6d8-0fd0749271a4\" />\r\n\r\n<img width=\"433\" height=\"120\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-05-24 at 11 08 51\u202fPM\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4c63ce57-5af7-4bc2-947a-89d262dbd350\" />\r\n\r\nThe branch and commit are also linked separately now. In the case where a commit is local but not on GitHub yet, I'm still using the \"dirty\" CSS styling (though the commit hash doesn't have the \"-dirty\" suffix). If you prefer that we just not handle that case I'm pretty ambivalent as well. Or maybe we could link to the most recent commit on GitHub as well as show the current commit hash?",
